.TH CJSON_CREATE_NULL_MP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_create_null_mp, cjson_create_bool_mp, cjson_create_int_mp, cjson_create_double_mp, cjson_create_string_mp, cjson_create_list_mp, cjson_create_dictionary_mp, cjson_create_null, cjson_create_bool, cjson_create_int, cjson_create_double, cjson_create_string, cjson_create_list, cjson_create_dictionary \- allocate DOM nodes with a custom or default allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

cjson cjson_create_null_mp(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cjson cjson_create_bool_mp(bool val, ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cjson cjson_create_int_mp(long long val, ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cjson cjson_create_double_mp(double val, ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cjson cjson_create_string_mp(const char *val, ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cjson cjson_create_list_mp(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cjson cjson_create_dictionary_mp(ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);

/* convenience wrappers; default allocator (NULL mp) */
static inline cjson cjson_create_null(void);
static inline cjson cjson_create_bool(bool val);
static inline cjson cjson_create_int(long long val);
static inline cjson cjson_create_double(double val);
static inline cjson cjson_create_string(const char *val);
static inline cjson cjson_create_list(void);
static inline cjson cjson_create_dictionary(void);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Each _mp function allocates a new DOM node of the matching JSON type,
with mp as the allocator.  A NULL mp means the default allocator,
which is malloc/free/calloc/realloc.  Each node keeps its own
allocator, and the library uses it for every later operation on the
node, including the destruction of the node.

The functions without _mp are static inline wrappers; each one calls
the _mp function with NULL as the allocator.

cjson_create_string_mp() makes a private heap copy of val.  A NULL
val gives a CJSON_NULL node, not a CJSON_STRING node that holds a
null pointer, because a null C string pointer maps to JSON null.  A
val that is not NULL must be valid UTF-8: JSON text is UTF-8 (RFC 8259
section 8.1), so any other byte sequence has no JSON form, and
cjson_create_string_mp() returns NULL for such a sequence instead of
storing it.  This matches the behaviour of cjson_create_double_mp()
for a double that is not finite.  To write a string field, use
cjson_set(), which applies the same rule but returns
ccol_invalid_args.
cjson_create_list_mp() returns an empty array; add children to it
with cjson_list_push().
cjson_create_dictionary_mp() returns an empty object; add children to
it with cjson_dictionary_set().  An empty array or object is one node,
and its first child allocates the storage that holds its children, so
the first push or set is the call that can fail for lack of memory.

cjson_create_double_mp() rejects a value that is not finite (Inf,
-Inf and NaN) and returns NULL for it, because JSON has no form for a
double that is not finite.  The parser also rejects a number that
overflows to infinity.  To write a double field, use cjson_set(),
which applies the same check and returns ccol_invalid_args on
failure.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp   Custom memory management procedures for this node and its owned
     resources, or NULL for the default allocator.
     The library keeps its own copy of the struct, so the caller may
     release or reuse the struct once this call returns, but the
     functions that it names must stay callable until the last node
     built with them is destroyed.  A process can use at most 64
     distinct sets of four functions; a call that would need a 65th
     fails and returns NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A new cjson node on success.  NULL when the allocation fails, when
val is not finite (cjson_create_double_mp), or when val is not valid
UTF-8 (cjson_create_string_mp).
cjson_create_string_mp(NULL, mp) returns a CJSON_NULL node; it never
returns NULL itself, except when it is out of memory.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The caller owns the node that the function returns and must free it
with cjson_destroy(), which uses the allocator that each node keeps
and so needs no mp parameter.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_destroy (3),
.BR cjson_list_push (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_set (3)
